{
 "what": "Replacement of .at() and substr() in include/ by span forms and operator[], with standard library hardening requested by the CMake target. bench/runtime.cpp arms S and READ on cwt, twitter and floats. before = include/ of d1f41df, after = this commit. ns per iteration and change against before in percent.",
 "why": "The owner decided that no throwing accessor stays in include/, and that standard library hardening checks the bounds that the code does not check itself.",
 "how": "g++-16 -std=c++23 -O2 -DNDEBUG. before, beforeAA and after with -D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS -D_LIBCPP_HARDENING_MODE=_LIBCPP_HARDENING_MODE_FAST, as the target now requests; after_nohard without them. beforeAA is a second build of before. Google Benchmark, --benchmark_min_time=0.05s, 11 rounds, all 24 binaries alternate in each round, 26.2 s, uid 1000, not pinned, nice 0. A run of 6 rounds before it (14.0 s) gave the same signs.",
 "noise": "A/A (beforeAA against before) is between -1.5 and +3.9 percent.",
 "finding": "after is not slower than before in any cell: S is 5 to 6 percent faster on all three documents, READ is 2 and 6 percent faster on cwt and twitter and +0.6 percent on floats, which is under the noise. after without hardening is not faster than after with hardening; 2 cells are slower than before by more than the noise (READ floats +14.6, S cwt +6.2). The cost of hardening is not measurable in this run.",
